commit 6cfb3b4be0bc03fc590eac6a603873adec48cf2c
Author: Arne Juul <arnej@vespa.ai>
Date:   Thu Sep 3 13:21:56 2026 +0000

    Allocate CUDA EP intermediates on their stream
    
    Einsum was the call site observed corrupting concurrent requests, but it was
    not the only one built the same way. Every Tensor::Create(type, shape,
    allocator) for a per-run intermediate reaches allocator->Alloc() with no
    stream, so the arena cannot associate the chunk with the stream that writes
    it, and is free to hand the memory to another stream the moment the tensor
    is released - possibly before queued work has completed.
    
    Sweep the CUDA EP and its contrib ops for that pattern and pass the stream
    that is already in scope at 22 remaining sites:
    
      Softmax transpose scratch and intermediate output
      Expand, Reshape output buffers
      Beam search TopK values and indices
      NCCL all-gather and sharding buffers, distributed Slice output
      Memcpy across devices for a tensor sequence (cuda_execution_provider.cc)
      IdentityOp and the four SequenceConstruct/Erase/Insert element copies
    
    The first sixteen are the hazard Einsum showed: scratch that goes out of
    scope inside Compute() while the copy or kernel writing it is still queued,
    so the chunk is back in the arena and reusable by another stream before the
    work that fills it has run.
    
    The last six are different and are fixed for consistency rather than to
    close a race. Their tensors are moved into the output TensorSeq, so they are
    not released mid-Compute and the end of run synchronization covers them.
    Tagging is still correct - a sequence output can outlive the run and be
    released by the caller at an arbitrary point - but the window Einsum hit is
    not open there.
    
    Conv and ConvTranspose PrePack are deliberately left alone: they run at
    session initialization on the default stream, and the transposed weights
    live as long as the kernel rather than going back to the arena between runs.
    
    Not covered here: generation_device_helper.cc allocates eight more per-run
    device buffers through Tensor::InitOrtValue(type, shape, allocator,
    ort_value) - the beam search past cache, input_ids, attention mask, cache
    indirection and ExpandBuffer's output - and each is written by an async
    memcpy or kernel on ort_stream, which is in scope at every one. They are the
    same hazard as the first group and churn harder, being reallocated every
    decode step, but there is no InitOrtValue overload that takes a stream.
    Adding one is a separate change.
    
    Four call sites in this sweep (softmax.cc x2, expand.cc, reshape.cc) tagged
    their Tensor::Create allocations with GetComputeStream() instead of
    GetAllocationStream(). Under BUILD_CUDA_EP_AS_PLUGIN, GetComputeStream()
    returns void* while Tensor::Create's stream-aware overload requires a
    Stream*, so these broke that build; they now use GetAllocationStream() to
    match einsum.cc and reduction_ops.cc. Also renamed ReduceComputeCore's
    compute_stream parameter to alloc_stream (it's only ever used for allocation
    tagging, never launch) so a future caller can't confuse it with the launch
    stream, and corrected GetAllocationStream's doc comment in cuda_kernel.h,
    which described the plugin adapter's null-return behavior rather than this
    implementation's.
    
    Add tests covering the intermediates a session can reach. The Einsum device
    helpers take their allocator and stream as arguments, so
    einsum_stream_allocation_test.cc calls them directly. The kernels swept here
    take theirs from OpKernelContext, so most of them can only be driven through
    a real Run(); add the two harnesses that reach them, and the header the
    Einsum test's recorder now lives in.
    
    cuda_kernel_stream_allocation_test.cc registers a cudaMalloc backed
    recording allocator as the environment's shared CUDA allocator, points a
    session at it with session.use_env_allocators, and records only the Run()
    window. There is no arena underneath, so every request is a distinct call
    and the counts are exactly the tensors the run allocated. Two cases:
    
      Softmax, measured differentially. The same model with the axis already
      innermost is the baseline; the transposed run has to add exactly two
      stream tagged allocations, the transpose scratch and the intermediate
      output.
    
      A SequenceConstruct/Insert/Erase/At chain, run with and without an
      Identity over the sequence so the difference isolates IdentityOp's element
      copies. That loop only runs when the kernel's output is not aliased onto
      its input, and being a graph output is what stops the planner applying the
      alias: AllocationPlanner tests for a graph output before it consults the
      alias map, so the output is planned kAllocateOutput and ExecutionFrame
      hands the kernel a fresh TensorSeq. Keeping SequenceAt as a second
      consumer of the same value is what stops EliminateIdentity rewiring the
      node away. A zero delta would mean the alias was applied after all and the
      case covered nothing, so it is an equality rather than a floor.
    
    Both cases also assert that no device allocation made during Run() came in
    through the untagged Alloc(). That is the assertion that catches any of
    these call sites reverting, and it reports the sizes it saw when it fails.
    
    beam_search_topk_stream_allocation_test.cc covers
    GenerationCudaDeviceHelper::TopK directly, since that one does take the
    allocator and the stream as arguments. Its two outputs are re-created every
    decode step, so the previous pair returns to the arena while TopKImpl may
    still be writing it.
    
    Left uncovered: the twelve NCCL, sharding and distributed Slice/Expand/Reshape
    sites, which need a multi-GPU NCCL build - FuncExpand and FuncReshape are only
    called from distributed_expand.cc and distributed_reshape.cc - and the
    TensorSeq branch of Memcpy in cuda_execution_provider.cc.
    
    All of these need onnxruntime_ENABLE_CUDA_EP_INTERNAL_TESTS=ON, and skip when
    no CUDA device is present.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

